{"subjectId":"java","id":"wait-notify","title":"wait/notify & Producer–Consumer","group":"Concurrency","order":60,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>wait()</code> and <code>notify()</code> let threads <em>coordinate</em> — sleep until a condition becomes true — instead of busy-spinning. In modern code a <code>BlockingQueue</code> usually does this for you.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Guarded wait</strong> — a thread holding an object's monitor calls <code>wait()</code> in a <code>while (!condition)</code> loop: it releases the monitor and sleeps until another thread calls <code>notify()</code>/<code>notifyAll()</code> on the same object, then re-acquires the monitor and re-checks the condition.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>The rules</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method (on <code>Object</code>)</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>wait()</code></td><td>Release this object's monitor and wait (state <code>WAITING</code>) until notified or interrupted</td></tr><tr><td><code>wait(ms)</code></td><td>Same, with a timeout (<code>TIMED_WAITING</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>notify()</code></td><td>Wake <em>one</em> arbitrary waiting thread</td></tr><tr><td><code>notifyAll()</code></td><td>Wake <em>all</em> waiting threads — they compete for the monitor and re-check their conditions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>All three must be called while holding the object's monitor (inside <code>synchronized (obj)</code>), otherwise <code>IllegalMonitorStateException</code>.</li><li>Always wait in a <strong><code>while</code></strong> loop, never <code>if</code>: threads can wake <em>spuriously</em>, and another thread may have consumed the condition first.</li><li>Prefer <code>notifyAll()</code> unless all waiters are interchangeable and only one can proceed.</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Object o = new Object();\n        try {\n            o.wait();                           // not holding o's monitor!\n        } catch (IllegalMonitorStateException | InterruptedException e) {\n            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>IllegalMonitorStateException</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i>A bounded buffer with wait/notifyAll</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        BoundedBuffer buf = new BoundedBuffer(2);\n        Thread producer = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            try {\n                for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) buf.put(i);\n                buf.put(-1);                              // poison pill: \"no more items\"\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n        });\n        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            try {\n                int sum = 0;\n                for (int x; (x = buf.take()) != -1; ) {\n                    System.out.println(\"consumed \" + x);\n                    sum += x;\n                }\n                System.out.println(\"sum = \" + sum);\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n        });\n        producer.start(); consumer.start();\n        producer.join();  consumer.join();\n    }\n}\n\nclass BoundedBuffer {\n    private final Queue&lt;Integer&gt; items = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n    private final int capacity;\n\n    BoundedBuffer(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }\n\n    synchronized void put(int x) throws InterruptedException {\n        while (items.size() == capacity) wait();   // full: wait for space\n        items.add(x);\n        notifyAll();                               // wake consumers\n    }\n\n    synchronized int take() throws InterruptedException {\n        while (items.isEmpty()) wait();            // empty: wait for data\n        int x = items.remove();\n        notifyAll();                               // wake producers\n        return x;\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>consumed 1\nconsumed 2\nconsumed 3\nconsumed 4\nconsumed 5\nsum = 15</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>The modern way: <code>BlockingQueue</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        BlockingQueue&lt;String&gt; queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(2);\n        Thread producer = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            try {\n                for (String job : new String[]{\"resize\", \"upload\", \"notify\"}) queue.put(job);\n                queue.put(\"DONE\");\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n        });\n        producer.start();\n        for (String job; !(job = queue.take()).equals(\"DONE\"); ) {\n            System.out.println(\"processing \" + job);\n        }\n        producer.join();\n        System.out.println(\"queue empty: \" + queue.isEmpty());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>processing resize\nprocessing upload\nprocessing notify\nqueue empty: true</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>BlockingQueue</code> op</th><th>Full / empty behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>put(e)</code> / <code>take()</code></td><td>Block until space / an element is available</td></tr><tr><td><code>offer(e)</code> / <code>poll()</code></td><td>Return <code>false</code> / <code>null</code> immediately</td></tr><tr><td><code>offer(e, t, unit)</code> / <code>poll(t, unit)</code></td><td>Block up to a timeout</td></tr><tr><td><code>add(e)</code> / <code>remove()</code></td><td>Throw an exception</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Implementations: <code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code> (bounded, array), <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code> (optionally bounded), <code>PriorityBlockingQueue</code>, <code>SynchronousQueue</code> (capacity 0 — a direct hand-off), <code>DelayQueue</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-off\"></i>Deadlock</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Deadlock</strong> — two or more threads each hold a lock the other needs, so all wait forever. It needs all four Coffman conditions: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait.</p></div>\n<p>Here T1 locks <code>a</code> then wants <code>b</code>, while T2 locks <code>b</code> then wants <code>a</code>. The latch makes the bad interleaving certain; the JVM's <code>ThreadMXBean</code> detects the cycle (the same thing <code>jstack</code> reports).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;\nimport java.lang.management.ThreadMXBean;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Object a = new Object(), b = new Object();\n        CountDownLatch bothHoldOne = new CountDownLatch(2);\n        Thread t1 = worker(\"T1\", a, b, bothHoldOne);\n        Thread t2 = worker(\"T2\", b, a, bothHoldOne);   // opposite order!\n        t1.start(); t2.start();\n        while (t1.getState() != Thread.State.BLOCKED || t2.getState() != Thread.State.BLOCKED) {\n            Thread.sleep(10);\n        }\n        ThreadMXBean mx = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean();\n        System.out.println(\"deadlocked threads: \" + mx.findDeadlockedThreads().length);\n        System.out.println(\"T1 waits for a lock owned by \" + mx.getThreadInfo(t1.threadId()).getLockOwnerName());\n        System.out.println(\"T2 waits for a lock owned by \" + mx.getThreadInfo(t2.threadId()).getLockOwnerName());\n    }\n\n    static Thread worker(String name, Object first, Object second, CountDownLatch latch) {\n        Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            synchronized (first) {\n                latch.countDown();\n                try { latch.await(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { return; }\n                synchronized (second) { System.out.println(name + \" finished\"); }\n            }\n        }, name);\n        t.setDaemon(true);                 // lets the JVM exit despite the deadlock\n        return t;\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>deadlocked threads: 2\nT1 waits for a lock owned by T2\nT2 waits for a lock owned by T1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i>Avoiding deadlock: a global lock order</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Account x = new Account(1, 1000), y = new Account(2, 1000);\n        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) transfer(x, y, 1); });\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) transfer(y, x, 2); });\n        t1.start(); t2.start();\n        t1.join();  t2.join();\n        System.out.println(\"x = \" + x.balance + \", y = \" + y.balance + \", total = \" + (x.balance + y.balance));\n    }\n\n    static void transfer(Account from, Account to, int amount) {\n        Account first = from.id &lt; to.id ? from : to;   // always lock the lower id first\n        Account second = first == from ? to : from;\n        synchronized (first) {\n            synchronized (second) {\n                from.balance -= amount;\n                to.balance += amount;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Account {\n    final int id;\n    int balance;\n    Account(int id, int balance) { this.id = id; this.balance = balance; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>x = 2000, y = 0, total = 2000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i>Lock ordering</h3><p>Acquire locks in one global order (by id, by <code>System.identityHashCode</code>) — breaks circular wait.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Timeouts</h3><p><code>ReentrantLock.tryLock(timeout)</code> — give up, release what you hold, retry.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-open\"></i>Fewer locks</h3><p>Hold one lock at a time; never call unknown (alien) code while holding a lock.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tools\"></i>Higher-level tools</h3><p>Concurrent collections, executors and immutable data remove most explicit locking.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Other liveness failures: <strong>livelock</strong> (threads keep reacting to each other and make no progress), <strong>starvation</strong> (a thread never gets the lock/CPU — e.g. unfair locks under heavy contention).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why must wait() be called inside a while loop?</summary><div><p>Because waking up does not mean the condition is true: <em>spurious wakeups</em> are allowed, <code>notifyAll()</code> wakes threads whose condition may still be false, and another thread may grab the monitor and consume the item first. Re-checking in a <code>while</code> handles all three.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>wait() vs sleep()?</summary><div><p><code>wait()</code> is an <code>Object</code> method, must hold that object's monitor, <em>releases</em> it and waits for a notify. <code>sleep()</code> is a static <code>Thread</code> method, needs no lock, and keeps all locks it holds. Both throw <code>InterruptedException</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why are wait/notify defined on Object and not Thread?</summary><div><p>Because they operate on a <em>monitor</em>, and every object is a monitor. Threads wait on the condition guarded by a particular object's lock, so the methods belong to the lock object.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you detect a deadlock in production?</summary><div><p>Take a thread dump (<code>jstack &lt;pid&gt;</code>, <code>jcmd &lt;pid&gt; Thread.print</code>, or VisualVM/JMC) — it prints \"Found one Java-level deadlock\" with the cycle. Programmatically, <code>ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}